WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.237
A useful way to think about a join is to separate three ideas: row sets, matching, and preservation.

00:00:07.237 --> 00:00:11.387
First, identify the two row sets being compared.

00:00:11.387 --> 00:00:15.882
For example, we may compare patients with admissions.

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:17.117
Second, define what makes two rows related.

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:24.202
In our healthcare example, an admission matches a patient when the patient IDs are equal.

00:00:24.202 --> 00:00:26.492
That matching rule belongs in the ON condition.

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:30.322
Third, decide what happens when a row has no match.

00:00:30.322 --> 00:00:33.357
That is the preservation question.

00:00:33.357 --> 00:00:37.302
INNER JOIN does not preserve unmatched rows.

00:00:37.302 --> 00:00:42.777
LEFT JOIN preserves unmatched rows from the left side.

00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:46.427
RIGHT JOIN preserves unmatched rows from the right side.

00:00:46.427 --> 00:00:50.507
FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both sides.

00:00:50.507 --> 00:00:56.147
This distinction is important because the ON condition and the JOIN type have different jobs.

00:00:56.147 --> 00:01:02.152
ON controls matching. The JOIN type controls which unmatched rows survive.

00:01:02.152 --> 00:01:08.622
Before you run a join, try to describe it in one sentence: compare these two row sets,

00:01:08.622 --> 00:01:12.568
match them using this rule, and preserve these unmatched rows.
